{
  "case_id": "2010-09-400",
  "document_hash": "01733025b4e1ca9ec2a13448287a45de59051e834ff3cb9d5dc3e3d0c46b1898",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Eric Schnapper)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-400",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "01733025b4e1ca9ec2a13448287a45de59051e834ff3cb9d5dc3e3d0c46b1898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-01733025b4e1-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The dismissal of an employee is often the result of the interrelated actions and decisions of several officials. Whether an employer is legally responsible for any particular official and his or her actions and decisions turns on agency law. Congress legislates against a background of agency law and is presumed to have intended agency principles to govern that kind of question. Agency law, not the Eleventh Circuit's \"cat's paw\" doctrine, is the controlling standard here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-09-400",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "01733025b4e1ca9ec2a13448287a45de59051e834ff3cb9d5dc3e3d0c46b1898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-01733025b4e1-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And the statute says that a prima facie case is made out if it is shown that military service, anti-military animus, was a motivating factor in the employer's action."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2010-09-400",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "01733025b4e1ca9ec2a13448287a45de59051e834ff3cb9d5dc3e3d0c46b1898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-01733025b4e1-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, that's a sweeping rule. I was going to ask a related hypothetical. Suppose the -- the officer who's in charge, charged with the decision to terminate or not to terminate, says: I'm going to have a hearing. You can both have counsel. And you have -- who is it -- suppose Buck -- suppose the two employees that were allegedly anti-military here testified, and they said there was no anti-military bias, and the person is then terminated. Later the employee has evidence that those two were lying. Could he bring an action then?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus) (Eric D. Miller)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-400",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "01733025b4e1ca9ec2a13448287a45de59051e834ff3cb9d5dc3e3d0c46b1898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-01733025b4e1-0000-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "An employer is liable under USERRA when a supervisor acting with a discriminatory motive uses a delegated authority to cause an adverse employment action. The court of appeals held that liability does not attach unless that supervisor exerts singular influence over the decisionmaker. But that standard is inconsistent with the statute for two reasons. First, it's incompatible with the statutory definition of \"employer,\" which includes not just the ultimate decisionmaker, but any person to whom the employer has delegated the performance of significant employment responsibilities."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-09-400",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "01733025b4e1ca9ec2a13448287a45de59051e834ff3cb9d5dc3e3d0c46b1898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-01733025b4e1-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: How do you get around the statutory language that says that the motivating -- it has to be a motivating factor in the -- in the action that is challenged?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. MILLER",
      "case_id": "2010-09-400",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "01733025b4e1ca9ec2a13448287a45de59051e834ff3cb9d5dc3e3d0c46b1898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-01733025b4e1-0001-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In the definition of \"employer\" in section 4303, it says that the employer includes everyone who has been delegated the performance of employment-related responsibilities."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-09-400",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "01733025b4e1ca9ec2a13448287a45de59051e834ff3cb9d5dc3e3d0c46b1898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-01733025b4e1-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: So you do accept that the traditional doctrine of an intervening cause is applicable in this?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. DAVIS",
      "case_id": "2010-09-400",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "01733025b4e1ca9ec2a13448287a45de59051e834ff3cb9d5dc3e3d0c46b1898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-01733025b4e1-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT\nMR. DAVIS: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court:\nThe parties to this case are in total agreement with respect to two points. The first point is that Linda Buck made the decision to fire Vincent Staub. And the second point is there is no evidence whatsoever that Linda Buck possessed animus toward Mr. Staub on account of his service in the Reserve."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amici (employers)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-400",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "01733025b4e1ca9ec2a13448287a45de59051e834ff3cb9d5dc3e3d0c46b1898",
      "evidence_id": "ev-01733025b4e1-0002-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "pointed out, amici on the other side, employers typically make a disciplinary decision as a result of a bunch of different decisions."
    },
